#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060516} :activity/id #uuid "2f099e95-7371-5eb4-a3d6-9210e6669092" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050713 :topic/id #uuid "60a435b6-b7ba-5949-9c3d-ce4c96d37141" :topic/math-academy-id 141} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060996 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the point $( - 1 , 2 )$ lies on the graph of $y = f ( x ).$ What point *must* lie on the graph of $y = - f ( x ) + 3?$ This problem can be solved using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - Transform the Point Using Graph Transformations**\n\nTo plot $y = -f(x) + 3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(2)) = (-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-2+3)={\\color{blue}(-1,1)}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2 - Evaluate the Transformed Function**\n\nSince the point $(-1, 2)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x),$ we have that $f(-1) = 2.$\n\nNow, consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ Substituting $x = {\\color{blue}-1}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+3 \\\\  & =-(2)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\color{blue}(-1, 1)$ must lie on $y = -f(x) + 3.$\n\nSo, both methods give the same result." :tutorial/id #uuid "1195d1cb-20e5-539e-beee-dfafe91b6953" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8400 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "17aa209f-3415-50cd-b096-6597119d3022" :step/math-academy-id 118630 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060517}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059668 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059434 :question/id #uuid "2138def0-a28a-5018-b93f-c63022825ecb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13411"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048753}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517170} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555831}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"} :step/id #uuid "ad5aa1ab-d653-5e01-bdaa-28b12af3c39a" :step/math-academy-id 118631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060518}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059435 :question/id #uuid "55fd531a-92e2-5f75-8c40-585d4f71bf4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13412"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517141} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555806}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"} :step/id #uuid "8380c8c1-7231-5bd4-9a45-21163a2d4d95" :step/math-academy-id 118634 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060998 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ shown below. \n\n![](images/af/afe8baa8375ceaf4974b62549e4f073d0e6cecfcfc1a50a91200421296141ef1.png)\n\n \n\nThis function has a local minimum point, and a local maximum point, as shown. \n\nNotice that if we perform the graph transformation $y=f(2x)-1,$ then \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *maximum* point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *minimum* point on the transformed curve.\n\n![](images/cd/cda6d2c00de091df0f4df03d4b63019fd7c5681159cff65ea25e7518f5e263cd.png)\n\n \n\nSo, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to maximums and minimums (local and global) to minimums. \n\nIs this always the case? In other words, does *any* graph transformation map maximums to maximums and minimums to minimums? \n\nTo answer this, let's consider another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d8a626b6-4914-5cb4-b571-7c88d5ccb12b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9005 :tutorial/title "Mapping Maxima and Minima"} :step/id #uuid "47250ab3-5cd2-5ed3-b989-5e178c31e80e" :step/math-academy-id 118637 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060521}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059436 :question/id #uuid "a0969b02-52c8-5d22-b3e0-b594d9fbd4d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13413"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517119} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517103}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"} :step/id #uuid "0eda5d7c-05ca-54a9-9399-5a17533e7389" :step/math-academy-id 118638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060999 :tutorial/content "Consider the transformation $y=-f(x)+2,$ which contains a reflection about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7f/7f4041c17b278b0e40e31abbd98e3c47a8462b14a6109123d600c2db0e5d6f36.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *minimum* ** point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *maximum* ** point on the transformed curve.\n\nSo, due to the presence of vertical reflection, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to minimums and minimums (local and global) to maximums." :tutorial/id #uuid "47145fae-178c-5567-b04b-16626d9cdea4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9037 :tutorial/title "Cases With Vertical Reflection"} :step/id #uuid "d663df39-763f-5675-9918-5cb19be43eb2" :step/math-academy-id 118832 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061000 :tutorial/content "We can summarize our findings by the following theorem:\n\n*If a transformation does **not** contain a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) maximums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) minimums. *\n\nIn the case where we do have a vertical reflection, we have the following theorem.\n\n*If a transformation contains a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) minimums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) maximums. *" :tutorial/id #uuid "38b1a98c-0588-5659-a4e5-2478a05f7a93" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9038 :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/id #uuid "5a7667c3-64c1-5b24-95d5-8fa128b323bd" :step/math-academy-id 118929 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060520}}] :activity/title "Finding Points on Transformed Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059878}]}
